ration, since those are easier to compare between implementations. I'm thinking about adding a few more test cases: one for the legacy `-t -d` flags with a custom template, another for dry-run mode with a custom template path, and maybe one more variant to round out the coverage.\n\nActually, let me add a test case where the Xs aren't at the end of the template—something like `fooXXXbar`—since that's a different kind of validation failure than just having too few Xs. This would strengthen the error case coverage.\n\nSetting up test cases... I'm working through how the normalizer handles the suffix case—the pattern matches exactly 10 alphanumeric characters after \"tmp.\", so it would correctly normalize `/tmpdir/tmp.aBcDeFgHiJ.txt` to `/tmpdir/tmp.XXXXXXXXXX.txt`. For the custom template test, the output would follow a similar pattern with the \"test\" prefix and 6 random characters.\n\nNow I'm checking the error case where `fooXXXbar` should be rejected—GNU mktemp counts only trailing X's, and since this has zero trailing X's, it fails the minimum requirement of 3. Both our implementation and GNU mktemp should produce the same error message after normalization.\n\nFor the `dry_run_with_suffix` test, I need to verify that the suffix normalizer correctly handles the `.txt` extension when matching the template output pattern. The template is actually a full path like `/var/folders/.../T/xxx/testXXXXXX`, so when it's passed as a positional argument to the binary with the `-u` flag, the parser treats it as a complete path rather than a relative template that would need the TMPDIR prefix added.\n\nIn the `run` function, since all the directory options are false or empty, `resolveTemplate` returns the path unchanged, then the code counts the 6 trailing Xs and generates random characters to print since dry-run mode is enabled. This should match what GNU mktemp does with the same arguments.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage